#!/bin/bash
# wg-hub/help.loc - Module-specific help

cat <<'EOF'
Module: wg-hub - hub side of a WireGuard network

  Generic. Nothing here is backup-specific; the same module is meant to serve
  a mobile/road-warrior network later, with different config and policy.

  WHY a given network is configured the way it is - threat model, isolation -
  lives in backup-box/README.md. This module is mechanism only.

SUBCOMMANDS

  setup                  Bootstrap the interface if absent, then sync peers
                         onto the LIVE interface and apply the LAN policy.
      yamc -h HUB -e net=bkup wg-hub              # report, then prompt
      yamc -h HUB -e net=bkup -e yes=1 wg-hub     # act

  status                 Interface, expected vs live peers, handshake ages,
                         policy state. Read-only.
      yamc -h HUB -e net=bkup wg-hub status

  -e net=NAME is REQUIRED. No default: the wrong one would apply a mobile
  network's policy to a backup network, or vice versa.

CONFIGURATION  (yamc.local/wgnet/<net>.conf and <net>.peers)

  WG_IFACE      interface name - must NOT start with 'wg' (see below)
  WG_PORT       listen port
  WG_SUBNET     e.g. 10.9.9.0/24
  WG_HUB_ADDR   this hub's tunnel address
  WG_HUB_PUBKEY printed on bootstrap; paste it in, peers need it
  WG_ENDPOINT   what peers dial (used by wg-peer)
  LAN_POLICY    none | route:<host> | full     REQUIRED, no default

  <net>.peers is pipe-delimited:   name | tunnel addr | public key | notes

  Duplicate addresses are refused. WireGuard does not error on overlapping
  AllowedIPs - it silently reassigns the address to whichever peer was
  configured last, misrouting the other.

LAN_POLICY IS FAIL-CLOSED

  Unset or unrecognised is a hard error, never a permissive default. Because
  this module is shared across networks, a permissive default is how a backup
  network would eventually get loosened by a change made for a mobile one.

    none          no forwarding at all; the tunnel terminates on the hub
    route:<host>  that one host may initiate to peers; peers may NEVER
                  initiate inward, only reply on established connections
    full          unrestricted LAN access (road warriors, not remote boxes)

  Rules go in their own nftables table at hook priority 100 - after iptables'
  filter FORWARD - so an ACCEPT installed by Docker or another instance
  cannot override them. The chain policy is ACCEPT and only this interface is
  restricted; a drop policy would break every other forwarding path on the
  host.

WHY THE INTERFACE IS NOT NAMED wg0

  Firewall snippets very commonly use the 'wg+' wildcard:

      iptables -A FORWARD -i wg+ -j ACCEPT

  That matches ANY interface starting with 'wg'. On a host already running
  another WireGuard instance, such a rule would silently grant forwarding to a
  new wg* interface too. The module REFUSES a WG_IFACE beginning with 'wg'.
  Hence names like 'bk0'. 'wg-hub status' also reports any wg+ rules it finds.

MANUAL EQUIVALENT

  Bootstrap, once:

    apt-get install wireguard-tools
    umask 077
    wg genkey | tee /etc/wireguard/bk0.key | wg pubkey > /etc/wireguard/bk0.pub

    cat > /etc/wireguard/bk0.conf <<'END'
    [Interface]
    Address    = 10.9.9.1/24
    ListenPort = 51822
    PrivateKey = <contents of bk0.key>
    PostUp     = nft -f /etc/wireguard/bk0.nft
    PostDown   = nft delete table inet wgnet_bkup 2>/dev/null || true
    END

    systemctl enable --now wg-quick@bk0

  A peer stanza (AllowedIPs MUST be a single /32 - WireGuard drops packets
  whose source is outside it BEFORE decryption, which is what makes address
  spoofing structurally impossible rather than merely filtered):

    [Peer]
    # orion
    PublicKey  = <peer public key>
    AllowedIPs = 10.9.9.2/32

  Add or revoke a peer, without dropping the interface:

    wg syncconf bk0 <(wg-quick strip bk0)

  syncconf REMOVES peers absent from the config - that is revocation, and it
  does not disturb the other peers.

  Inspect:  wg show bk0

BEFORE FIRST USE

  Audit the host - see backup-box/README.md, "Pre-Build Audit". Check for wg+
  wildcard rules, confirm net.ipv4.ip_forward is already on (so isolation
  cannot rest on it being off), and confirm the new subnet does not overlap an
  existing one.

  Also make sure UDP <WG_PORT> is forwarded to this host at the border router.

SEE ALSO

  wg-peer                the spoke side
  backup-box/README.md   system design and threat model
EOF
